    "facts": "The petitioner was convicted and sentenced to undergo simple imprisonment for a period of six months under Section 16 (1)(a)(i) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (for short, 'the Act'), and to pay fine of ` 1,000/- and in default of payment of fine, to undergo further simple imprisonment for a period of two months.\nThe milk fat fell below minimum specified limit of 4% and the milk solids not fat fell below minimum specified limit of 8.5%.\nThe petitioner was ordered to be summoned for the commission of offence under Section 7 punishable under Section 16(1)(a)(i) of the Act.\nThe petitioner appeared in the Court and contested the case.\n3. The prosecution examined a number of witnesses in support of the case.\nHe was convicted and 2 of 5 Criminal Revision No. 1269 of 2008 (3) sentenced by the learned trial Court vide judgment dated 23.11.2005, as noticed above.\nThe sample was sent to Public Analyst, Karnal.\nThe same was not found to be conforming to the standards laid down for the cow milk in the table.\n3 of 5  Criminal Revision No. 1269 of 2008 (4)\nParamjit Singh vs State Of Haryana on 31 May, 2019 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/26899133/\n2 7.\nThe sample did not conform to the standards prescribed.\nThe petitioner had waited for more than six years till 12.4.2002 for sending the second sample.\nHe was required to do so within 10 days from the receipt of notice from the Local Health Authority, Kalka.\n10. The prosecution though has not examined Gurnam Singh but the statement of the official witnesses inspire confidence.\n",
